lesson:
```
int scores[4] = {72, 85, 90, 64};
scores[0];                        /* 72 */
scores[3] = 66;                   /* change one */
int n = sizeof(scores) / sizeof(scores[0]);   /* 4, but ONLY where the array was made */

int total = 0;
for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) total += scores[i];
```

An array can't grow: you choose the size when you make it. (Python's lists and C++'s vectors are built on top of arrays that get swapped for bigger ones when full.)

**Arrays don't carry their length.** When you pass one to a function, it arrives as just the address of the first item, so you always pass the count too:

```
double average(const int a[], int n)
{
    if (n == 0) return 0;
    int total = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) total += a[i];
    return (double)total / n;
}
```

`const` promises the function won't change the array.

**Nothing checks the index.** `scores[10]` on a 4-item array reads (or worse, writes) whatever memory comes next. This "buffer overflow" is the source of countless crashes and security holes, so check your loop bounds.

example.code:
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void)
{
    int scores[4] = {72, 85, 90, 64};
    int n = sizeof(scores) / sizeof(scores[0]);
    int total = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) total += scores[i];
    printf("%d scores, total %d, average %.2f\n", n, total, (double)total / n);
    return 0;
}

